1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:04,888
Every few nights, survey telescopes photograph the same patches of sky.

2
00:00:04,888 --> 00:00:07,513
Almost nothing in those pictures changes.

3
00:00:07,513 --> 00:00:14,025
What we're after is the little that does: here, a new point of light where there was none, and a star that got brighter.

4
00:00:14,925 --> 00:00:18,163
So why not subtract last night's picture from tonight's?

5
00:00:18,163 --> 00:00:19,662
Here's what that gives you.

6
00:00:19,662 --> 00:00:22,662
Every star leaves a bright half and a dark half.

7
00:00:22,662 --> 00:00:27,900
The telescope pointed a few pixels differently, and turned a little, so nothing lines up.

8
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:30,750
First, fix the geometry.

9
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:37,312
The file headers say where every pixel points on the sky, so we can resample one image onto the other's grid.

10
00:00:37,312 --> 00:00:39,713
In cuPhoton, that's xRep.

11
00:00:39,713 --> 00:00:41,137
Now subtract again.

12
00:00:41,137 --> 00:00:44,025
Better, but every star still leaves a ring.

13
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:46,550
The rings come from the air.

14
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:49,463
On one night it blurred starlight more than on the other.

15
00:00:49,463 --> 00:00:53,137
Subtract a narrow bump from a wide one and you get this shape.

16
00:00:53,137 --> 00:00:57,187
To cancel it, blur the sharper image until it matches the other.

17
00:00:57,187 --> 00:01:03,000
xPois fits that blur, called a kernel, together with the brightness scale and the sky level.

18
00:01:03,925 --> 00:01:13,063
Now the unchanged stars cancel into noise, and two things stand out: a star that got three times brighter, and a source that wasn't there at all.

19
00:01:13,063 --> 00:01:18,875
The empty frame around the edge is where the blur can't be matched: there's no data beyond the border.

20
00:01:19,775 --> 00:01:20,362
Align.

21
00:01:20,362 --> 00:01:22,000
Match and subtract.

22
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:22,763
Find.

23
00:01:22,763 --> 00:01:23,400
Measure.

24
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:24,337
Decide.

25
00:01:24,337 --> 00:01:27,225
That's the core of the pipeline you'll direct in this course.

26
00:01:27,225 --> 00:01:29,287
Your agent will do the typing.

27
00:01:29,287 --> 00:01:33,725
Your job is to know what each picture should look like, and to notice when it doesn't.
